From: Manuel de Brito Fontes <aledbf@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 14:00:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 7/11] x86/unwind/orc: Build the lookup table in one pass

unwind_init() fills orc_lookup[] with one binary search of the whole
.orc_unwind_ip table for every 256-byte block of kernel text. On this
kernel that is 59,650 searches of 415,913 entries, about a million
dependent loads on the boot CPU before the memory allocator is up.

Both the table, sorted at build time by sorttable, and the block start
addresses ascend, so walk them together instead: each block's entry is
the rightmost one at or below its start address, found by moving forward
from the previous block's. The lookup table it builds is the one the
binary searches built, including the rightmost-duplicate choice
__orc_find() makes so that a section terminator does not hide the real
entry at the same address, and the first entry for a block below the
first ORC address.

Checked on this kernel with a late initcall that redid the binary
search for every block and compared (an experiment build, not part of
this patch): 59,650 blocks, 0 differ; the merge pass took 0.56 ms and
the binary searches 3.12 ms. dump_stack() from that initcall and
/proc/self/stack in the guest still unwind.

Measured in a KVM guest on a laptop, not nested (QEMU q35, two vCPUs,
two runs of 10 boots each, interleaved; a lead, not the bench fleet):
the time from "refined-jiffies" to "Zone ranges", which is
unwind_init(), 4.59 -> 0.93 and 4.45 -> 0.72 ms p50. On the bench
fleet the gap was 3.5 ms nested and on bare metal alike (lab runs
44563496308, 44563512999): it is CPU, not exits.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 arch/x86/kernel/unwind_orc.c | 42 +++++++++++++++++++-----------------------
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+), 23 deletions(-)

--- a/arch/x86/kernel/unwind_orc.c
+++ b/arch/x86/kernel/unwind_orc.c
@@ -335,8 +335,8 @@ void __init unwind_init(void)
 	size_t orc_ip_size = (void *)__stop_orc_unwind_ip - (void *)__start_orc_unwind_ip;
 	size_t orc_size = (void *)__stop_orc_unwind - (void *)__start_orc_unwind;
 	size_t num_entries = orc_ip_size / sizeof(int);
-	struct orc_entry *orc;
-	int i;
+	unsigned int i, j = 0;
+	unsigned long ip;
 
 	if (!num_entries || orc_ip_size % sizeof(int) != 0 ||
 	    orc_size % sizeof(struct orc_entry) != 0 ||
@@ -348,31 +348,27 @@ void __init unwind_init(void)
 	/*
 	 * Note, the orc_unwind and orc_unwind_ip tables were already
 	 * sorted at build time via the 'sorttable' tool.
-	 * It's ready for binary search straight away, no need to sort it.
+	 *
+	 * Initialize the fast lookup table in one pass over both: the block
+	 * start addresses ascend, and so do the entries, so each block's
+	 * entry is found by moving forward from the previous block's. Each
+	 * block gets the rightmost entry at or before its start address, or
+	 * the first entry if there is none, which is what __orc_find() would
+	 * return for it. The last block is the end of the last range.
 	 */
-
-	/* Initialize the fast lookup table: */
 	lookup_num_blocks = orc_lookup_end - orc_lookup;
-	for (i = 0; i < lookup_num_blocks-1; i++) {
-		orc = __orc_find(__start_orc_unwind_ip, __start_orc_unwind,
-				 num_entries,
-				 LOOKUP_START_IP + (LOOKUP_BLOCK_SIZE * i));
-		if (!orc) {
-			orc_warn("WARNING: Corrupt .orc_unwind table.  Disabling unwinder.\n");
-			return;
-		}
-
-		orc_lookup[i] = orc - __start_orc_unwind;
-	}
+	for (i = 0; i < lookup_num_blocks; i++) {
+		if (i < lookup_num_blocks - 1)
+			ip = LOOKUP_START_IP + (LOOKUP_BLOCK_SIZE * i);
+		else
+			ip = LOOKUP_STOP_IP;
+
+		while (j + 1 < num_entries &&
+		       orc_ip(__start_orc_unwind_ip + j + 1) <= ip)
+			j++;
 
-	/* Initialize the ending block: */
-	orc = __orc_find(__start_orc_unwind_ip, __start_orc_unwind, num_entries,
-			 LOOKUP_STOP_IP);
-	if (!orc) {
-		orc_warn("WARNING: Corrupt .orc_unwind table.  Disabling unwinder.\n");
-		return;
+		orc_lookup[i] = j;
 	}
-	orc_lookup[lookup_num_blocks-1] = orc - __start_orc_unwind;
 
 	orc_init = true;
 }
